{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>Unstable as water, etc.</b> Meaning: you were unstable in troubling my couch and did not subdue your inclination, but you rushed like an impulsive man and did not restrain yourself to overcome the evil spark that had taken root in you; therefore you shall have no superiority — of all the three superiorities — and this is the plain sense." "Further, he alluded in His saying <b>אַל תּוֹתַר</b> that the impetuousness he practiced — doing an improper thing — he did not practice in a matter in which there is superiority. For regarding repentance, he did not precede to repent, as they said of blessed memory (Bava Kama 92b): who caused Reuben to confess? — Judah. And although they said of blessed memory in this language: there was no man who began with repentance until Reuben came, etc. — even so, confession he did not utter until Judah came and said ״צָדְקָה מִמֶּנִּי״; at that moment Reuben burst forth and said ״אֲנִי בִּלְבַּלְתִּי מִטַּת אָבִי״ — thus it is brought in the Midrash (Tanchuma), for so is the tradition in their hands. And according to their words here, we may say that for this reason Reuben's kingship was given to Judah on account of the completion of the confession that they caused Reuben to make. And the priesthood that was given to Levi — it appears that he did not merit it except through the agency of his deeds afterward, as it says (Deuteronomy 33:8): ״לְאִישׁ חֲסִידֶךָ אֲשֶׁר נִסִּיתוֹ בְּמַסָּה״ etc., and only then was the priesthood given to Levi. In any event, from the days of Jacob all the superiorities were taken from Reuben, as he said <b>אַל תּוֹתַר</b>, and Jacob gave two superiorities: one to Joseph and one to Judah, and the third remained suspended and standing until the Holy One, blessed be He, gave it to the one worthy of it whom He tested, etc." "<b>And corresponding</b> to what touches upon the act of building his root, he said <b>כִּי עָלִיתָ</b> etc. — meaning: the reason for your doing so is that at the time when his seed ascended into his mother's womb, it was in a state of commotion during its ascent, for he ascended upon two beds, Leah and Rachel — one in deed and one in thought, for in deed it was Leah and in thought Jacob intended that she was Rachel; and this is what is meant by <b>מִשְׁכְּבֵי אָבִיךָ</b>. And this was the reason that <b>then</b> you profaned my couch — he ascended, for the aspect of evil gives birth in its own likeness: just as he ascended in commotion, so he troubled his father's couch. And therefore Scripture was precise in saying <b>אָז</b> (then), to allude that it is not about the time when he ascended upon his father's couch that he says he profaned my couch, for they are two separate times: the time of ascending upon the couch is the time when he ascended into his mother's womb, and the time of profaning was afterward."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:50:26.391691220Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}